window.ESSAY_BANK5 = [

/* ================== CHAPTER 1 ================== */
{ id:"K1-01", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Explain the SIC classification of investment banks and what it tells us about the scope of the industry.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Under the US <b>Standard Industrial Classification</b>, investment banks fall within industry <b>6211</b> — &ldquo;Security Brokers, Dealers, and Flotation Companies&rdquo; (major group 62). It covers establishments engaged in the <b>purchase, sale and brokerage</b> of securities, and those &ldquo;generally known as investment bankers&rdquo; engaged in <b>originating, underwriting and distributing</b> issues of securities; commodity contracts brokers and dealers form a separate industry (6221).</p><p><b>What it tells us.</b> Three things. First, the industry is defined by <b>securities activity</b> rather than by deposit-taking or lending — the classification places it alongside brokers and dealers, not banks. Second, the pairing of &ldquo;purchase and sale&rdquo; with &ldquo;brokerage&rdquo; captures the fundamental duality of the business: <b>agency</b> work earning fees and commissions, and <b>principal</b> work earning spreads and trading gains. Third, the breadth of the description — originating, underwriting, distributing, buying, selling, brokering — shows that the narrow definition of investment banking as underwriting alone is incomplete even in official taxonomy.</p>",
  rubric:"Correct groups identified (0.6) · own account / others' account duality (0.7) · implication that scope exceeds underwriting alone (0.7)." },

{ id:"K1-02", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain why an investment bank's balance sheet expands and contracts with market activity, and why this matters.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The bank raises funding only against actual investment or transaction needs — the <b>liability-management</b> orientation. When activity is high it warehouses underwriting positions, holds market-making inventory, extends margin loans and finances client positions, funding all of this largely through <b>repo and other secured borrowing</b> against those very assets. When activity falls, the positions are unwound and the associated funding is simply not renewed. The balance sheet therefore breathes with market conditions rather than being anchored by a stable deposit base.</p><p><b>Why it matters.</b> The funding is short-term and continuously rolled, so the bank depends on daily access to the secured funding market. That access rests on two conditions: the collateral must remain liquid and reliably valued, and counterparties must remain confident in the firm's name. In a crisis both fail at once — collateral values become uncertain precisely when confidence evaporates. The bank cannot shrink an illiquid asset book fast enough to match, so a solvent institution can fail on liquidity within days. This is the mechanism that destroyed Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers in 2008.</p>",
  rubric:"Liability-management mechanism with examples of assets funded (0.8) · dependence on rolling secured funding (0.5) · simultaneous failure of collateral and confidence, with the 2008 illustration (0.7)." },

{ id:"K1-03", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the difference between an agency function and a principal function, and give two examples of each with the associated risk.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> In an <b>agency</b> function the bank acts <i>for</i> a client, executing or advising on the client's behalf. It takes no position, so it bears no market risk; its exposure is to <b>operational, execution and conduct</b> risk, and it is paid a fee or commission. Examples: <b>brokerage</b> — executing client orders for commission, exposed to mis-execution and best-execution failure; and <b>M&amp;A advisory</b> — advising on a transaction for a fee, exposed to reputational damage and liability for negligent advice.</p><p>In a <b>principal</b> function the bank acts <i>for itself</i>, committing its own balance sheet. It holds positions and bears <b>market risk</b>, and is paid through a spread or trading gain. Examples: <b>market making</b> — quoting two-sided prices and holding inventory, exposed to inventory and adverse-selection risk; and <b>underwriting on a firm-commitment basis</b> — buying an entire issue for resale, exposed to placement risk if the issue cannot be sold at the expected price.</p><p>The distinction matters because the duties differ fundamentally: as agent the bank owes loyalty, best execution and client priority; as principal it is the client's counterparty, and its gain is the client's cost.</p>",
  rubric:"Clear distinction with risk and revenue type (0.8) · two valid agency examples with risk (0.5) · two valid principal examples with risk (0.5) · duty implication (0.2)." },

{ id:"K1-04", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Describe three conflicts of interest that arise between divisions of an investment bank and one control for each.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>(1) Research versus the investment-banking division.</b> Analysts face pressure to publish favourable reports on companies the bank is advising or underwriting, in order to win or protect the mandate. <i>Control:</i> research reporting lines and compensation separated from banking revenue, no pre-publication review by bankers, and mandatory disclosure of banking relationships in every report.</p><p><b>(2) Research versus proprietary trading.</b> Analysts may be pressured to publish views that benefit the firm's own positions — for example a buy recommendation while the desk is selling. <i>Control:</i> the information barrier plus surveillance of proprietary trading around the timing of research publication.</p><p><b>(3) The investment-banking division versus investment management.</b> When the bank underwrites an issue it has an interest in placing it, including into portfolios it manages for other clients. <i>Control:</i> restrictions or outright prohibition on placing own-underwritten securities into discretionary client portfolios, with disclosure and independent suitability testing where permitted.</p><p>(A fourth valid answer is proprietary trading versus brokerage — the front-running risk — controlled by client order priority and segregated systems and personnel.)</p>",
  rubric:"Three genuine conflicts correctly described (1.2) · a specific and appropriate control for each (0.8)." },

{ id:"K1-05", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what the Dodd-Frank Act requires of investment banks and why each requirement was introduced.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)</b> was the deepest regulatory change to US financial services since 1934, enacted in response to the 2007–08 crisis. Its principal requirements for investment banks are:</p><p><b>(1) Capital.</b> Banks must support their assets with at least 5% equity. <i>Why:</i> pre-crisis leverage of 30× or more meant a small fall in asset values wiped out equity; more capital absorbs loss before failure.</p><p><b>(2) Liquidity.</b> Much higher holdings of highly liquid assets convertible quickly into cash. <i>Why:</i> the crisis was a funding run — firms were unable to roll short-term secured borrowing and had no liquid buffer to survive the gap.</p><p><b>(3) Annual stress tests.</b> Firms must demonstrate survival of a simulated crisis at least as severe as 2008 without government support. <i>Why:</i> to end &ldquo;too big to fail&rdquo; bailouts by identifying weakness in advance.</p><p><b>(4) The Volcker Rule</b> restricting most proprietary trading. <i>Why:</i> to prevent deposit-taking groups using the public safety net to fund speculative positions.</p><p>Broader objectives were consumer protection, coordination between regulators, early identification of systemic risk, and transparency of complex instruments and executive compensation. The collective effect was to lower permissible leverage and therefore structurally reduce industry ROE.</p>",
  rubric:"At least three requirements correctly stated (1.0) · a valid rationale for each (0.7) · ROE/leverage consequence (0.3)." },

{ id:"K1-06", ch:1, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what a 'restricted list' and a 'watch list' are and how they differ.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Both are maintained by the <b>control room</b> within compliance to manage the risk arising from material non-public information held on the private side.</p><p>A <b>restricted list</b> names securities in which the firm's activity is actively <b>curtailed</b>: research may not be published or ratings changed, proprietary trading is prohibited, and solicitation of client orders may be restricted. A security is placed on it when the firm has a live mandate — for example an announced or imminent transaction — and the restriction is <b>visible to the relevant staff</b>, because they must comply with it.</p><p>A <b>watch list</b> is <b>confidential</b> and is not circulated to business staff. It records securities on which the firm holds sensitive information, and compliance uses it to <b>monitor</b> trading and research activity for signs of misuse. Its confidentiality is the point: publishing it would itself signal that a transaction is under way, which could move the market and breach the client's confidence.</p><p>In short: the restricted list <b>prevents</b> activity and is disclosed internally; the watch list <b>detects</b> misuse and is kept secret.</p>",
  rubric:"Restricted list purpose and visibility (0.8) · watch list purpose and confidentiality (0.8) · the prevent-versus-detect distinction (0.4)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 2 ================== */
{ id:"K2-01", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Explain the three components into which the gross spread is divided and what each rewards.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The gross spread — the difference between the offer price and the proceeds paid to the issuer — is conventionally split three ways.</p><p><b>(1) Management fee.</b> Paid to the lead manager or bookrunner for <b>running the transaction</b>: structuring, coordinating the syndicate, managing due diligence and documentation, building and controlling the book, and setting the price and allocations. It rewards origination and execution effort.</p><p><b>(2) Underwriting fee.</b> Paid to syndicate members in proportion to their underwriting commitments, rewarding them for <b>assuming placement risk</b> — the obligation to purchase securities they cannot resell. It compensates the use of the balance sheet and is the component most sensitive to deal risk.</p><p><b>(3) Selling concession.</b> Paid to whichever syndicate or selling-group member <b>actually places</b> the shares with an investor. It rewards distribution effort and is typically the largest of the three.</p><p>The bookrunner takes the largest overall share because it controls the process, the book and the allocation.</p>",
  rubric:"Three components correctly named (0.6) · what each rewards (0.9) · bookrunner's larger share or the risk-linkage point (0.5)." },

{ id:"K2-02", ch:2, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"A company offers 12 million shares at VND 48,000 with a 6.5% gross spread and a 15% greenshoe fully exercised. Compute total shares, gross proceeds, the fee and net proceeds.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Greenshoe shares = 15% × 12,000,000 = <b>1,800,000</b>.<br>Total shares = 12,000,000 + 1,800,000 = <b>13,800,000</b>.</p><p>Gross proceeds = 13,800,000 × 48,000 = <b>VND 662.4 billion</b>.</p><p>Underwriting fee = 6.5% × 662.4bn = <b>VND 43.056 billion</b>.</p><p>Net proceeds to the issuer = 662.4 − 43.056 = <b>VND 619.344 billion</b>.</p><p>Note that exercising the greenshoe increased the issuer's gross proceeds by 1.8m × 48,000 = VND 86.4bn, so the option benefits the issuer as well as stabilising the aftermarket — it is exercised only when the share price has risen above the offer price.</p>",
  rubric:"Greenshoe and total shares (0.5) · gross proceeds (0.5) · fee (0.5) · net proceeds (0.3) · observation on the greenshoe's effect (0.2)." },

{ id:"K2-03", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the conditions a Vietnamese joint-stock company must satisfy to make a public offering of shares.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Under the <b>Law on Securities 2019</b> the principal conditions are:</p><ul><li>Paid-up <b>charter capital of at least VND 30 billion</b> at the time of registration, measured at book value;</li><li><b>Profitable operations in the two preceding years</b>, with no accumulated losses up to the year of registration;</li><li>An <b>offering plan and plan for the use of proceeds</b> approved by the General Meeting of Shareholders;</li><li><b>Ownership dispersion</b> — at least 15% of voting shares sold to at least 100 investors who are not major shareholders (10% where charter capital is VND 1,000 billion or more);</li><li>A commitment by <b>major shareholders</b> to hold at least 20% of charter capital for at least one year from completion;</li><li>The issuer is <b>not under criminal prosecution</b> or serving a criminal judgment for an economic offence;</li><li>An <b>securities company advising on the registration dossier</b>, unless the issuer is itself a securities company (an adviser is required, not an underwriter); and a commitment to <b>list or register for trading</b> after the offering;</li><li>An account to receive and hold the proceeds.</li></ul><p>Candidates should verify current thresholds against the prevailing law and guiding decrees, since they are periodically amended.</p>",
  rubric:"At least five conditions with correct figures where applicable (1.6) · advising securities company or listing commitment (0.2) · caveat on verifying current law (0.2)." },

{ id:"K2-04", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what determines the coupon on a corporate bond at issue.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The coupon is built from two elements: a <b>benchmark yield</b> and a <b>credit spread</b>.</p><p>The <b>benchmark</b> is the yield on a government bond of comparable maturity, representing the risk-free rate for that term. It reflects monetary policy, inflation expectations and the shape of the yield curve — factors entirely outside the issuer's control.</p><p>The <b>credit spread</b> is the premium demanded for lending to this issuer rather than the government. It is determined principally by the <b>credit rating</b>, which summarises the probability of default and the expected recovery. It is also affected by the <b>structure</b> — maturity, seniority, whether the debt is secured, the strength of covenants, and any call or conversion features — and by <b>market conditions</b>: liquidity, investor appetite and the state of the credit cycle.</p><p>So: <b>coupon ≈ benchmark yield + credit spread</b>. For example, a benchmark of 4.2% plus a 260bp spread gives approximately 6.8%. This is why rating advisory is a core part of DCM work: an upgrade narrows the spread and reduces funding cost across all future issuance.</p>",
  rubric:"Benchmark component with its drivers (0.6) · credit spread with rating as the primary driver (0.7) · structural and market factors (0.4) · the additive relationship or a worked figure (0.3)." },

{ id:"K2-05", ch:2, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the difference between a strategic investor and a financial investor in a private placement, and the implications for the issuer.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> A <b>strategic investor</b> is usually an operating company in a related industry. Its motive is <b>industrial</b>: access to a market, technology, distribution or supply, and it expects to hold the stake indefinitely. A <b>financial investor</b> — a private equity or venture fund — invests for <b>financial return</b> over a defined horizon, typically three to seven years, and requires an identifiable exit.</p><p><b>Implications for the issuer.</b> A strategic investor may pay a <b>higher price</b>, because it can capture synergies a financial investor cannot, and it brings operational expertise, market access and credibility. But it may also seek influence over strategy, restrict the company's freedom to deal with its competitors, and it may ultimately want full control — the minority stake becomes a route to acquisition.</p><p>A financial investor generally brings <b>governance discipline</b>, professional reporting and capital-markets expertise, and does not compete with the company. But it will press for value creation on its own timetable, will negotiate protective rights such as liquidation preference and board representation, and — critically — <b>its exit must be planned for</b>, whether by IPO, trade sale or buy-back, which shapes the company's medium-term strategy.</p>",
  rubric:"Motive and horizon of each correctly contrasted (0.8) · at least two implications of a strategic investor (0.6) · at least two implications of a financial investor incl. exit (0.6)." },

{ id:"K2-06", ch:2, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"A startup is valued at VND 90bn pre-money and raises VND 30bn. (a) Compute the post-money valuation and the investor's stake. (b) Founders held 100% before; what do they hold after, and what is it worth?",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Post-money = pre-money + investment = 90 + 30 = <b>VND 120 billion</b>.<br>Investor's stake = 30 ÷ 120 = <b>25%</b>.</p><p>(b) The founders are diluted from 100% to <b>75%</b>. Their holding is worth 75% × VND 120bn = <b>VND 90 billion</b> — exactly the pre-money valuation.</p><p>This is the essential logic of a priced equity round: the founders' <i>percentage</i> falls but the <i>value</i> of their holding is unchanged at the moment of investment, because the company has received VND 30bn of new cash in exchange for the 25% issued. Dilution is only economically harmful if the company is valued too cheaply — that is, if the pre-money valuation is set below fair value, in which case the founders transfer value to the investor. This is why negotiating the pre-money valuation, rather than the percentage, is the substance of a funding round.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) post-money and stake (0.8) · (b) founders' percentage and value (0.7) · insight that value is preserved unless the pre-money valuation is understated (0.5)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 3 ================== */
{ id:"K3-01", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Explain the difference between a merger, a consolidation and an acquisition under Vietnamese enterprise law.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Merger (sáp nhập).</b> One or more companies transfer all of their assets, rights, obligations and lawful interests to another <b>existing</b> company, and then cease to exist. The receiving company survives and succeeds to everything transferred — A + B → B.</p><p><b>Consolidation (hợp nhất).</b> Two or more companies combine to form an entirely <b>new</b> company, to which all assets, rights and obligations pass; <b>all</b> of the original companies cease to exist — A + B → C.</p><p><b>Acquisition (mua lại).</b> One company purchases all or part of another's <b>shares or assets</b> in a proportion sufficient to control it. The target does <b>not</b> necessarily cease to exist — it commonly continues as a subsidiary with its own legal personality — A buys control of B, B survives.</p><p>The practical distinctions matter for whether the target's legal personality survives, whether liabilities transfer automatically by operation of law, what corporate approvals are required, and whether competition clearance is triggered.</p>",
  rubric:"Three forms correctly defined with the survival outcome of each (1.5) · practical significance of the distinction (0.5)." },

{ id:"K3-02", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"Explain what a control premium is, why buyers pay it, and how an advisor tests whether it is justified.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>control premium</b> is the amount by which the offer price exceeds the target's <b>unaffected</b> share price — the price prevailing before any rumour or announcement of the transaction. For listed targets it commonly runs at 20–40%.</p><p><b>Why buyers pay it.</b> Control confers rights a minority holder does not have: the ability to direct strategy, appoint management, set the capital structure and dividend policy, access and redeploy the target's cash flows, and — decisively — to <b>realise synergies</b> that only a controlling owner can implement. The premium is the price of those rights.</p><p><b>How it is tested.</b> The advisor values the target <b>standalone</b>, then values the <b>synergies separately</b> — net of the costs to achieve them and on a realistic phasing — and discounts them to present value. The premium is justified only if <b>PV(net synergies) exceeds the premium paid</b>. Since the acquirer contributes the synergies, a disciplined buyer aims to retain part of that value rather than paying the seller for all of it. The advisor should also cross-check the implied multiple against <b>precedent transactions</b>, which embed control premia, and stress-test the synergy assumptions in a downside case.</p>",
  rubric:"Definition incl. the unaffected price (0.5) · rights conferred by control and the synergy link (0.7) · the PV(synergies) > premium test with costs to achieve (0.8)." },

{ id:"K3-03", ch:3, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"midterm",
  q:"A target has 40m shares trading at an unaffected price of VND 35,000, and net debt of VND 400bn. An acquirer offers a 28% premium. Compute the offer price, the equity purchase price and the implied enterprise value.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Offer price per share = 35,000 × 1.28 = <b>VND 44,800</b>.</p><p>Equity purchase price = 44,800 × 40,000,000 = <b>VND 1,792 billion</b>.</p><p>Implied enterprise value = equity purchase price + net debt = 1,792 + 400 = <b>VND 2,192 billion</b>.</p><p>Note why net debt is <b>added</b>: the acquirer takes on the target's debt as well as buying its equity, so the true cost of acquiring the operating business is the enterprise value, not the equity price. This is also the figure that must be compared with EBITDA to compute the implied EV/EBITDA multiple and to benchmark the deal against precedent transactions.</p>",
  rubric:"Offer price (0.6) · equity purchase price (0.6) · enterprise value with net debt added (0.5) · explanation of why net debt is added (0.3)." },

{ id:"K3-04", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what a data room is, what it contains and how access is controlled.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> A <b>data room</b> — now almost always a secure <b>virtual</b> data room — is the controlled repository through which a seller makes due-diligence material available to shortlisted bidders in the second round of an auction.</p><p><b>Contents.</b> Corporate documents (constitution, share register, group structure); audited and management financial statements with supporting schedules; the operating model and budgets; material contracts with customers, suppliers, lenders and landlords; employment and pension arrangements; intellectual property and licences; litigation and regulatory correspondence; tax filings; environmental and technical reports; and insurance policies.</p><p><b>Access control.</b> Entry follows execution of a <b>non-disclosure agreement</b>. Permissions are set by bidder and by folder, so competitively sensitive material — customer names, pricing, key contract terms — is withheld or redacted until late in the process, particularly where the bidder is a competitor. Activity is <b>logged</b>, showing which documents each bidder viewed and for how long, which also tells the seller how seriously each is engaging. Documents may be watermarked and download-disabled, and a <b>clean team</b> arrangement may be used so that only designated advisors, not the competitor's operational staff, see the most sensitive data.</p>",
  rubric:"Definition and place in the process (0.5) · at least five categories of content (0.7) · NDA, staged permissions and logging (0.6) · clean team or redaction for competitors (0.2)." },

{ id:"K3-05", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the difference between representations and warranties, indemnities, and conditions precedent in a purchase agreement.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Representations and warranties</b> are statements of fact made by the seller about the target — that the accounts are accurate, that it owns its assets, that there is no undisclosed litigation. They allocate the risk of <b>unknown</b> problems: if a warranty proves untrue the buyer has a claim for damages, measured by the reduction in the target's value. They are qualified by disclosure, by materiality thresholds and by caps and time limits.</p><p><b>Indemnities</b> address <b>known or specifically identified</b> risks — a pending tax assessment, an environmental liability, a live dispute discovered in diligence. The seller undertakes to reimburse the buyer pound-for-pound if the risk crystallises, without the buyer having to prove loss of value. Indemnities are therefore the response to something diligence has <i>found</i>, whereas warranties protect against what it might have <i>missed</i>.</p><p><b>Conditions precedent</b> are requirements that must be <b>satisfied before completion can occur</b> — competition clearance, sector-regulator approval, shareholder consent, third-party change-of-control consents, or the absence of a material adverse change. They are not compensation mechanisms; if a condition is not met, the transaction simply does not close.</p><p>In short: warranties compensate for unknown defects, indemnities for identified ones, and conditions precedent determine whether the deal happens at all.</p>",
  rubric:"Warranties as risk allocation for unknowns with a damages remedy (0.7) · indemnities for identified risks with pound-for-pound recovery (0.7) · conditions precedent as gating items (0.4) · summarising distinction (0.2)." },

{ id:"K3-06", ch:3, type:"essay", clo:"CLO1", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain why an LBO sponsor prefers a target with low capital expenditure requirements.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> In a leveraged buyout the acquisition debt is serviced from the <b>target's own cash flow</b>. Every unit of cash the business generates has competing claims on it: interest payments, scheduled amortisation, working-capital needs, and <b>capital expenditure</b>.</p><p>Capital expenditure is largely <b>non-discretionary</b> in the medium term — maintenance capex must be spent to keep the asset base functioning, and cutting it merely defers the requirement while degrading the business the sponsor intends to sell. So a capital-hungry target consumes precisely the cash the structure needs for <b>debt service and paydown</b>.</p><p>The consequences are twofold. First, <b>less debt capacity</b>: lenders size the facilities against cash flow available after capex, so a high-capex business supports lower leverage and therefore requires a larger equity cheque, reducing the sponsor's return. Second, <b>slower deleveraging</b>: since debt paydown is typically the single largest contributor to LBO equity returns, cash diverted to capex directly reduces the money multiple and IRR.</p><p>A low-capex business with stable cash flow can therefore support both higher leverage and faster deleveraging — which is why the textbook lists low capital-expenditure requirements among the core target-selection criteria, alongside strong predictable cash flow, a defensible market position and a proven management team.</p>",
  rubric:"Competing claims on cash flow incl. capex as non-discretionary (0.7) · reduced debt capacity and larger equity cheque (0.6) · slower deleveraging reducing returns (0.5) · link to selection criteria (0.2)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 4 ================== */
{ id:"K4-01", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the role of the supervisory bank (custodian) in a public fund and why its independence matters.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>supervisory bank</b> holds the fund's assets in <b>custody, separately from the assets of the fund management company</b>, and monitors the manager's compliance with the fund charter, the prospectus and the applicable regulations. It typically also verifies the calculation of NAV, confirms that subscriptions and redemptions are processed correctly, and reports breaches to the regulator.</p><p><b>Why independence matters.</b> The arrangement separates <b>decision-making</b> from <b>possession</b>. The manager decides what to buy and sell but never holds the assets or the cash; the custodian holds them but has no discretion over investment. Neither party can therefore misappropriate client assets alone — a fraud would require collusion between two separately regulated institutions.</p><p>This structure also protects investors against the <b>insolvency</b> of the fund management company: because the assets are held by the custodian in the fund's name and segregated from the manager's own balance sheet, they are not available to the manager's creditors. Independence is what makes the custodian's compliance monitoring credible — a supervisor within the same group, or dependent on the manager for its revenue, could not meaningfully challenge it.</p>",
  rubric:"Custody and compliance-monitoring roles (0.8) · separation of decision-making from possession, requiring collusion for fraud (0.7) · insolvency protection through segregation (0.5)." },

{ id:"K4-02", ch:4, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"A fund has AUM of VND 2,400bn, charges 1.8% management fee and 20% carry over a 9% hurdle, and returns 16% gross. Compute the performance fee percentage, the investor's net return and the manager's total revenue.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Excess return over the hurdle = 16% − 9% = <b>7%</b>.</p><p>Performance fee = 20% × 7% = <b>1.4%</b> of AUM.</p><p>Investor's net return = 16% − 1.8% − 1.4% = <b>12.8%</b>.</p><p>Manager's revenue:<br>Management fee = 1.8% × 2,400bn = <b>VND 43.2 billion</b><br>Performance fee = 1.4% × 2,400bn = <b>VND 33.6 billion</b><br>Total = <b>VND 76.8 billion</b>.</p><p>Note that the total fee load of 3.2% consumed one fifth of the gross return. Had the fund returned only 9%, no carry would have arisen and the investor would have netted 7.2% — while the manager would still have collected VND 43.2bn in management fees. This asymmetry is why AUM, rather than performance, is the primary driver of an asset manager's revenue.</p>",
  rubric:"Excess and performance fee percentage (0.6) · net return (0.5) · both revenue components and total (0.6) · observation on fee load or the below-hurdle case (0.3)." },

{ id:"K4-03", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what tracking error measures and why it is the key metric for both passive and active managers, in different ways.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Tracking error</b> is the standard deviation of the difference between the portfolio's returns and the benchmark's returns — that is, the volatility of <b>active return</b>. It measures how far, and how consistently, the portfolio deviates from its benchmark.</p><p><b>For a passive manager</b> it is the primary measure of <b>success or failure</b>. The objective is replication, so any deviation is error: a well-run index fund should show tracking error of only a few basis points, arising from fees, cash drag, sampling and transaction costs. High tracking error in a passive fund means it is failing at its only job.</p><p><b>For an active manager</b> tracking error is not error but <b>the risk budget</b>. Deviation from the benchmark is the entire point — a manager who does not deviate cannot outperform. Here it is the <b>denominator</b> of the information ratio, IR = active return ÷ tracking error, which measures how efficiently the manager converts active risk into active return. It is also a <b>mandate constraint</b>: an IPS may cap tracking error, and exceeding it is a compliance breach regardless of the return achieved.</p><p>So the same statistic is a measure of failure in one context and of deliberate, budgeted risk-taking in the other.</p>",
  rubric:"Correct definition as volatility of active return (0.6) · passive interpretation as error with typical magnitude (0.5) · active interpretation as risk budget, IR denominator and mandate limit (0.7) · the contrast drawn (0.2)." },

{ id:"K4-04", ch:4, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the difference between an advisory mandate and a discretionary mandate, and when each is appropriate.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Under an <b>advisory mandate</b> the manager <b>recommends</b> and the client <b>decides</b>: no transaction occurs without the client's specific instruction. Under a <b>discretionary mandate</b> the manager may <b>trade without pre-approval</b> of each transaction, within the objectives, constraints and permitted ranges agreed in the investment policy statement.</p><p><b>When advisory is appropriate.</b> Where the client has the knowledge, time and appetite to be involved; where they wish to retain control over each decision; where holdings carry personal significance — a founder's stake, an inherited holding; and where the relationship is being established and trust is still developing.</p><p><b>When discretionary is appropriate.</b> Where <b>timely execution</b> matters and delay would harm the client — rebalancing in volatile markets, acting on a fast-moving opportunity; where the client lacks the time or expertise; where the portfolio is complex or spans many markets; and where consistency across many similar clients is required.</p><p>The critical point is that discretion does <b>not</b> mean unlimited authority. It operates strictly within the IPS: anything outside the agreed objectives, constraints, permitted instruments or ranges requires the client's specific authority and an amendment to the IPS. Acting outside it is a mandate breach whether or not the outcome was favourable.</p>",
  rubric:"Both mandates correctly defined (0.7) · appropriate circumstances for each (0.8) · discretion bounded by the IPS and breach point (0.5)." },

{ id:"K4-05", ch:4, type:"case", clo:"CLO2", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"A fund manager's largest holding falls 40% after an accounting scandal. Redemption requests surge. Analyse the manager's obligations and the options available.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — obligations.</b> (1) <b>Fair treatment of all unitholders</b>, both those redeeming and those remaining. Meeting redemptions by selling the most liquid holdings first would leave continuing investors with a concentrated, illiquid residue — a transfer of value from those who stay to those who leave. (2) <b>Accurate valuation</b>: the affected holding must be valued properly, and if it is suspended or its price is unreliable, a fair-value process with the supervisory bank's involvement is required — striking NAV on a stale price would let redeemers exit at an overstated value. (3) <b>Disclosure</b> to investors and the regulator of the position and its impact. (4) <b>Adherence to the fund charter</b> on redemption terms and any liquidity tools. (5) <b>Review of the investment process</b> — why was the position so large, and were concentration limits observed?</p><p><b>Options.</b> Meet redemptions from <b>cash and liquid buffers</b> first, if adequate. Sell assets on a <b>pro-rata slice</b> basis across the portfolio rather than only the liquid names, preserving the mix for continuing holders. Apply <b>swing pricing</b> so that the dealing costs caused by redeeming investors are borne by them rather than by those remaining. If the outflow threatens orderly management, apply a <b>redemption gate</b> limiting the proportion redeemed in a period, or in extremis <b>suspend dealing</b> — but only if the charter permits it, with prompt regulatory notification and clear communication, since suspension is a serious step that traps investors.</p><p><b>Judgement.</b> The manager should act on valuation first, then meet redemptions in the way that least disadvantages continuing investors, escalating to gates or suspension only when orderly realisation is genuinely impossible. The episode should also trigger a review of concentration limits, since the underlying failure was allowing a single holding to become large enough to cause this.</p>",
  rubric:"Fair-treatment obligation with the value-transfer mechanism (0.8) · valuation and disclosure duties (0.6) · at least three options incl. pro-rata slicing and swing pricing (0.9) · gates/suspension as last resort with charter and regulator (0.4) · concentration-limit review (0.3)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 5 ================== */
{ id:"K5-01", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain Porter's five forces and how the framework informs a valuation.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The five forces determine the structural profitability of an industry:</p><ul><li><b>Rivalry among existing competitors</b> — intensified by high fixed costs, undifferentiated products, many similar-sized players and slow growth.</li><li><b>Threat of new entrants</b> — restrained by barriers such as capital requirements, scale economies, brand, regulation and control of distribution.</li><li><b>Threat of substitutes</b> — alternative ways for the customer to meet the same need, which caps the price the industry can charge.</li><li><b>Bargaining power of buyers</b> — strong where buyers are few, large, price-sensitive and face low switching costs.</li><li><b>Bargaining power of suppliers</b> — strong where suppliers are concentrated, their input is critical, or switching is costly.</li></ul><p><b>How it informs a valuation.</b> The forces determine whether an industry can <b>sustain</b> above-normal returns, and therefore discipline the model's key assumptions. Weak rivalry and high entry barriers support assumptions of durable margins and a long fade period before returns decline to the cost of capital — which directly supports a higher <b>terminal growth rate</b> and terminal value. Strong buyer power or a serious substitute threat implies margin compression, so forecast margins should decline and the terminal assumptions should be conservative. A DCF projecting expanding margins in an industry with intensifying rivalry and falling barriers is internally inconsistent — the framework is what exposes that inconsistency.</p>",
  rubric:"Five forces correctly named with a driver for each (1.0) · link to sustainability of returns (0.4) · explicit connection to margin, fade and terminal assumptions (0.6)." },

{ id:"K5-02", ch:5, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"A company has net income of VND 420bn, revenue VND 3,500bn, total assets VND 2,800bn and equity VND 1,400bn. Compute the DuPont components and ROE, and comment on the quality of the return.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> Net margin = 420 ÷ 3,500 = <b>12.0%</b><br>Asset turnover = 3,500 ÷ 2,800 = <b>1.25×</b><br>Equity multiplier = 2,800 ÷ 1,400 = <b>2.0×</b></p><p>ROE = 12.0% × 1.25 × 2.0 = <b>30.0%</b><br><i>Check:</i> 420 ÷ 1,400 = 30.0% ✓</p><p><b>Comment.</b> A 30% ROE is strong, and the decomposition shows it is <b>well balanced</b>: a healthy 12% net margin combined with efficient asset use at 1.25× turnover, and only <b>moderate</b> leverage of 2.0× (equivalent to debt being half of total assets). The return is therefore driven principally by <b>operating performance</b> rather than by financial gearing.</p><p>This matters for quality. A company reporting the same 30% ROE from a 6% margin, 1.0× turnover and 5.0× leverage would be generating its return largely through balance-sheet risk — vulnerable to a downturn, to rising interest rates and to covenant pressure. Comparing two firms on ROE alone would rank them equally; DuPont reveals that this company's return is more sustainable and lower risk.</p>",
  rubric:"Three components computed (0.9) · ROE with cross-check (0.4) · interpretation identifying operating rather than leverage-driven return (0.4) · quality contrast with a leveraged alternative (0.3)." },

{ id:"K5-03", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the concept of the equity value bridge and why each adjustment is made.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The bridge converts <b>enterprise value</b> — the value of the operating business, belonging to all providers of capital — into <b>equity value</b>, which belongs to shareholders alone.</p><p><b>Equity value = EV − net debt − minority interest − preferred + non-operating assets</b></p><ul><li><b>Less net debt</b> (total debt minus cash): debtholders rank ahead of shareholders, so their claim is deducted. Cash is netted off because it could be used to repay debt.</li><li><b>Less minority interest</b>: where the company consolidates a subsidiary it does not wholly own, EV reflects 100% of that subsidiary's operations, but the portion attributable to outside shareholders does not belong to the parent's equity holders.</li><li><b>Less preferred stock</b>: preferred ranks ahead of common equity and is economically debt-like.</li><li><b>Plus non-operating assets</b>: surplus land, investment portfolios or minority stakes generate no EBITDA, so a multiple applied to operating EBITDA does not capture them. They must be added at fair value or they would be valued at zero.</li></ul><p>Dividing by <b>diluted</b> shares — including options, warrants and convertibles that are in the money — then gives value per share. The governing principle is <b>consistency</b>: any item whose income is excluded from the earnings measure must be valued separately, and any claim ranking ahead of common equity must be deducted, or the result double-counts.</p>",
  rubric:"Correct bridge formula (0.5) · reason for each of net debt, minorities and preferred (0.8) · non-operating assets added with the reason (0.4) · diluted shares and the consistency principle (0.3)." },

{ id:"K5-04", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what the conglomerate discount is, why it arises, and how it is remedied.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> The <b>conglomerate discount</b> is the tendency of a diversified group to trade at a market value <b>below the sum of the values of its individual businesses</b> if each were valued on its own sector's multiples.</p><p><b>Why it arises.</b> (1) <b>Opacity</b> — consolidated reporting makes it hard for investors to assess each business separately, and cross-subsidy between divisions can conceal weak performers. (2) <b>Investors can diversify more cheaply themselves</b> by holding several focused companies, so they will not pay a premium for a diversification they do not need. (3) <b>Capital misallocation</b> — internal capital markets may fund weaker divisions that would fail to attract external finance. (4) <b>Management attention is spread thin</b> across unrelated businesses. (5) Analysts and index funds specialise by sector, so a conglomerate attracts a narrower and less expert investor base.</p><p><b>Remedies.</b> The analytical starting point is a <b>sum-of-the-parts (SOTP)</b> valuation, valuing each division on its own peer multiples and aggregating, to quantify the gap. The structural responses are then a <b>spin-off</b> (distributing a subsidiary's shares to existing holders, raising no cash but letting the market value it independently), an <b>equity carve-out</b> (selling a minority stake by IPO, raising cash and establishing a market price while retaining control), or an outright <b>divestiture</b> for cash. Improved segmental disclosure can narrow, though rarely eliminate, the discount.</p>",
  rubric:"Definition (0.4) · at least three causes (0.8) · SOTP as the quantifying tool (0.3) · at least two structural remedies correctly described (0.5)." },

{ id:"K5-05", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain why a company might trade below its book value, and what an analyst should investigate.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> A price-to-book ratio below 1.0 means the market values the company at less than its accounting net assets. There are three broad explanations.</p><p><b>(1) Returns below the cost of equity.</b> This is the most common and most rational reason. If ROE is persistently below the cost of equity, each unit of book equity <b>destroys</b> value, so the market rationally discounts it. A firm earning 5% on equity against a 13% cost of equity should trade well below book — the residual income model formalises this.</p><p><b>(2) Assets are overstated.</b> Book value may not reflect economic reality: receivables may be uncollectible, inventory obsolete, goodwill from past acquisitions impaired but not yet written down, or fixed assets carried above recoverable value. The market may simply be marking assets the accounts have not.</p><p><b>(3) Distress, governance or liquidity.</b> Expected financial distress, doubts about minority shareholders' access to the assets, or a very thin free float can all depress price independently of asset quality.</p><p><b>What to investigate.</b> Compare ROE with the cost of equity over several years; test asset quality — receivable ageing, inventory turnover, goodwill impairment testing, the age and utilisation of fixed assets; check for off-balance-sheet or contingent liabilities; assess leverage, covenant headroom and refinancing risk; and examine governance and related-party transactions. The key question is whether the discount reflects a <b>genuine value problem</b> or a <b>mispricing</b> — only the latter is an opportunity.</p>",
  rubric:"ROE below cost of equity as the primary explanation (0.7) · asset overstatement (0.4) · distress/governance/liquidity (0.3) · at least four specific investigations and the closing judgement (0.6)." },

{ id:"K5-06", ch:5, type:"essay", clo:"CLO2", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the difference between operational restructuring and capital restructuring, with two examples of each.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Operational (corporate) restructuring</b> changes <b>what the company owns and how it is organised</b> — the asset side and the business portfolio. Its purpose is to sharpen strategic focus, exit underperforming or non-core activities, and allow the market to value the remaining businesses properly. <i>Examples:</i> a <b>divestiture</b>, selling a non-core division for cash; and a <b>spin-off</b>, distributing a subsidiary's shares to existing shareholders so that two differently-valued businesses can be priced independently. (Carve-outs, split-offs and liquidation are equally valid.)</p><p><b>Capital restructuring</b> changes the <b>financing mix</b> — the liability and equity side — usually without altering the operating business at all. Its purpose is to optimise the cost of capital, match the maturity and currency of liabilities to the assets they fund, restore flexibility, or return capital to shareholders. <i>Examples:</i> a <b>dividend recapitalisation</b>, borrowing to fund a large distribution to shareholders; and a <b>debt-for-equity swap</b>, in which creditors exchange claims for shares to reduce an unsustainable debt burden. (Refinancing, share buybacks and rights issues are equally valid.)</p><p>The essential distinction: operational restructuring changes the <b>business</b>; capital restructuring changes only <b>who finances it and on what terms</b>. The two are often combined — a divestiture may fund a debt reduction — but the analytical questions they answer are different.</p>",
  rubric:"Both types correctly defined by which side of the balance sheet they affect (0.8) · two valid examples of each (0.8) · the summarising distinction (0.4)." },

/* ================== CHAPTER 6 ================== */
{ id:"K6-01", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain the difference between price priority and time priority, and how a call auction price is determined.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> In continuous matching the exchange applies two rules in sequence. <b>Price priority</b> comes first: among buy orders the <b>highest</b> bid is matched first, and among sell orders the <b>lowest</b> ask — the most aggressive prices trade first, since they are most willing to transact. <b>Time priority</b> then breaks ties: among orders at the <b>same</b> price, the one entered <b>earlier</b> is matched first. This ordering rewards both aggressive pricing and early commitment of liquidity, and it makes the queue predictable and fair.</p><p><b>Call auction.</b> Rather than matching continuously, orders accumulate over a period and are matched at a <b>single price</b>. The system determines the price at which the <b>greatest volume can be executed</b>; where several prices would produce the same volume, tie-breaking rules apply, typically minimising the surplus imbalance and referencing the previous price. All trades then execute at that one price regardless of the limits individually submitted, so buyers bidding above it and sellers offering below it receive price improvement. <b>ATO</b> orders participate only in the opening auction and <b>ATC</b> orders only in the closing auction, and these take precedence over limit orders at the same price.</p>",
  rubric:"Price priority stated correctly for both sides (0.6) · time priority as tie-breaker (0.4) · volume-maximising auction price and single execution price (0.7) · ATO/ATC precedence (0.3)." },

{ id:"K6-02", ch:6, type:"calc-essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"A stock's reference price is VND 84,000 with a ±7% band. (a) Compute the ceiling and floor. (b) The stock opens limit-up and stays there with no sellers. Explain the consequence for investors.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> (a) Ceiling = 84,000 × 1.07 = <b>VND 89,880</b><br>Floor = 84,000 × 0.93 = <b>VND 78,120</b><br>(Both are then rounded to the applicable tick size.)</p><p>(b) &ldquo;Limit-up with no sellers&rdquo; means the price has reached the ceiling of VND 89,880 and there is buying interest at that price but no one willing to sell. The consequences are:</p><ul><li><b>Almost no trading occurs.</b> The quoted price is at the ceiling but volume is negligible, so the price is not a genuine market-clearing price — it is simply the highest the rules permit.</li><li><b>Buyers cannot obtain stock</b> at any price the rules allow, however much they are willing to pay. Their orders queue by time priority.</li><li><b>Price discovery is delayed.</b> If material news justifies a move larger than 7%, the market cannot express it in one session; the stock may lock limit-up for several consecutive sessions until the price approaches fair value.</li><li><b>Liquidity is impaired precisely when it is most needed.</b> The mirror case, limit-down with no buyers, is more serious: holders cannot exit at any price, and leveraged investors facing margin calls cannot sell to meet them, so collateral cannot be realised.</li></ul><p>This is the fundamental trade-off of price bands: they dampen disorderly volatility and give participants time to absorb information, but at the cost of delaying genuine price discovery and, in extreme cases, trapping investors.</p>",
  rubric:"(a) both figures (0.6) · (b) negligible volume and inability to transact (0.6) · delayed price discovery over multiple sessions (0.4) · the limit-down mirror case and margin implication (0.4)." },

{ id:"K6-03", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain what a central counterparty does and how it reduces risk in securities settlement.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> A <b>central counterparty (CCP)</b> interposes itself between the two sides of every trade through <b>novation</b>: the original contract between buyer and seller is replaced by two contracts, so the CCP becomes <b>buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer</b>.</p><p><b>How this reduces risk.</b> (1) <b>Counterparty risk is transformed.</b> Each participant is exposed only to the CCP — a heavily regulated, well-capitalised institution — rather than to a large number of individual counterparties whose creditworthiness it cannot assess. (2) <b>Multilateral netting</b> dramatically reduces the number and value of obligations to be settled: a participant's many purchases and sales in the same security net to a single delivery obligation, cutting both settlement volume and liquidity requirements. (3) <b>Margining</b> — the CCP collects initial margin against potential future exposure and variation margin against daily price movements, so losses are collateralised before they crystallise. (4) A <b>default fund</b> and a defined default waterfall — the defaulter's margin first, then its contribution, then the CCP's own capital, then mutualised contributions — absorb a member failure in an orderly way. (5) <b>Standardisation</b> of processes and timetables reduces operational risk.</p><p>The caveat is that risk is <b>concentrated</b> rather than eliminated: the CCP itself becomes systemically critical, which is why its margin models, membership standards and default arrangements are so closely regulated.</p>",
  rubric:"Novation correctly explained (0.6) · at least three risk-reduction mechanisms incl. netting and margining (0.9) · default waterfall or standardisation (0.2) · concentration caveat (0.3)." },

{ id:"K6-04", ch:6, type:"essay", clo:"CLO3", marks:2, exam:"final",
  q:"Explain why securities lending exists from the lender's perspective, and the risks the lender accepts.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer.</b> <b>Why lenders lend.</b> The typical lender is a long-term holder — a pension fund, mutual fund, insurer or custodian acting for them — holding securities it does not intend to sell. Lending generates an <b>incremental borrow fee</b> on assets that would otherwise sit idle, improving the portfolio's return at apparently low effort. The fee can be material for scarce, hard-to-borrow stock. Lenders also receive <b>collateral</b> — cash or high-quality securities, typically exceeding the value of the loaned stock — which can itself be reinvested for additional return, and they retain the <b>economic</b> benefits of ownership, since dividends are passed through by the borrower.</p><p><b>Risks accepted.</b> (1) <b>Counterparty risk</b> — the borrower may fail to return the securities; collateral must then be liquidated, and it may prove insufficient if prices have moved. (2) <b>Collateral reinvestment risk</b> — historically the largest source of loss: cash collateral reinvested in instruments that fall in value or become illiquid leaves the lender short even though the stock lending itself performed as intended. (3) <b>Loss of voting rights</b> during the loan, since legal title passes to the borrower — a governance concern if a contested vote arises, requiring recall. (4) <b>Operational and legal risk</b> in recall, corporate actions and tax treatment. (5) <b>Reputational risk</b>, since the lender is facilitating short selling of a security it holds, which may conflict with its own or its clients' interests.</p>",
  rubric:"Fee income on idle assets with collateral and dividend pass-through (0.7) · at least three risks incl. counterparty and collateral reinvestment (0.9) · loss of voting rights or reputational point (0.4)." },

{ id:"K6-05", ch:6, type:"case", clo:"CLO3", marks:3, exam:"final",
  q:"A retail client complains that their leveraged position was force-sold at the market low without adequate warning, and demands compensation. Analyse the firm's position and what should have been in place.",
  guide:"<p><b>Model answer — analysing the firm's position.</b> The firm's defence rests on whether it followed a <b>documented, contractually agreed process</b>. The key questions are: (1) Did the <b>margin agreement</b> clearly disclose the initial and maintenance margin requirements, the circumstances in which a call would be made, the time allowed to respond, and the firm's right to liquidate <b>without further notice</b> if the call was unmet? (2) Was the client's <b>understanding assessed</b> at onboarding — were they suitable for leveraged trading, and was risk disclosure given and acknowledged? (3) Was the <b>call actually issued</b>, through the agreed channels, with an auditable record of despatch and timing? (4) Was the <b>liquidation itself</b> conducted properly — only to the extent necessary to restore the ratio, at prices obtainable in the market, without preferring the firm's own interests?</p><p>If all of these are documented and were followed, the client's loss is the consequence of leverage they contracted for, and compensation is not owed — the force-sale occurring at a market low is inherent to the pro-cyclical nature of margin lending, not evidence of fault. If any link is missing — particularly proof that the call was sent and time allowed — the firm is exposed.</p><p><b>What should have been in place.</b> A clear, signed margin agreement with explicit liquidation rights; suitability assessment and risk warnings at onboarding, appropriate to a retail client; automated <b>intra-day monitoring</b> with alerts to the client as the equity ratio approaches the maintenance level, not only once breached; a documented <b>call procedure</b> with defined timelines and multiple contact channels, fully logged; a <b>liquidation policy</b> specifying that only the necessary amount is sold, with records of execution prices against market prices at the time; conservative loan-to-value ratios and an eligible-securities list limiting exposure to volatile stock; and a complaints procedure. The wider lesson is that margin lending's legal defensibility rests almost entirely on the quality of the audit trail.</p>",
  rubric:"At least three of the four key questions identified (1.0) · reasoned conclusion linking documentation to liability (0.6) · at least five specific controls that should have existed (1.0) · audit-trail lesson (0.4)." }

];
